{%- comment -%}
  Quick view — the panel's contents, split out so the section can render them
  in the product's own context.

  theme.js asks Shopify for `<product url>?section_id=<this section>` and swaps
  the result into the overlay, exactly as the bag drawer is re-rendered after a
  cart change. Money, translation and image sizing therefore stay in Liquid, and
  the panel is a rendering of the product rather than a copy of it assembled in
  the browser.

  Accepts:
    product - product object.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign gallery_images = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'image'
  assign product_videos = product.media | where: 'media_type', 'video'
  assign gallery_video = product.metafields.custom.spin_360_video.value | default: product_videos.first

  assign slide_count = gallery_images.size
  if gallery_video != blank
    assign slide_count = slide_count | plus: 1
  endif

  comment
    The design's framing correction, matched exactly as the card matches it —
    earrings before rings, because "earrings" contains "ring".
  endcomment
  assign zoom_base = blank
  if settings.card_image_zoom
    assign zoom_base = product.metafields.custom.card_zoom.value
    if zoom_base == blank
      assign zoom_match = product.type | append: ' ' | append: product.title | downcase
      assign zoom_base = 1.15
      if zoom_match contains 'bracelet'
        assign zoom_base = 1.05
      elsif zoom_match contains 'earring'
        assign zoom_base = 1.39
      elsif zoom_match contains 'ring'
        assign zoom_base = 1.32
      endif
    endif
  endif

  assign category = product.type
  if category == blank
    assign category = product.collections.first.title
  endif

  assign current_variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant

  comment
    The axes.

    `GV.groups()` hands the design's quick view one row per axis and it renders
    whatever comes back, never naming an axis itself: "Pages never hard-code an
    axis: they render whatever `groups()` hands back, which is what lets a ring
    show seven choices and a pair of earrings show six without either page
    knowing." (gj-variants.js.) Here the axes are the product's own options and
    the variants are Shopify's, so the prices are the ones checkout will charge
    rather than a multiplier table's.

    Two options are singled out, by the same names the rest of the theme matches
    them on — see snippets/line-options.liquid. The metal is the row that gets
    swatches and the design's own ordering; the karat is composed into the metal
    row's caption, because "18K Yellow Gold" is one fact about the piece, which
    is exactly what the design's `metalName()` builds and what a bag line shows.
  endcomment
  assign metal_option = blank
  assign karat_option = blank
  assign karat_index = 0
  assign has_options = false

  unless product.has_only_default_variant
    assign has_options = true
    for option in product.options_with_values
      assign option_down = option.name | downcase
      if option_down contains 'purity' or option_down contains 'karat'
        if karat_option == blank
          assign karat_option = option
          assign karat_index = forloop.index0
        endif
      elsif option_down contains 'metal'
        if metal_option == blank
          assign metal_option = option
        endif
      endif
    endfor
  endunless

  capture ordered_metals_raw
    render 'metal-order', option: metal_option
  endcapture
  assign ordered_metals = ordered_metals_raw | strip | split: '||'

  comment
    Which options are painted with swatches, worked out once.

    An option gets the swatch treatment when it is the metal, or when its values
    carry a native Shopify swatch of their own — never because of a table of
    metal names kept here, which is the rule metal-swatch.liquid is written to.
    The flags are computed here rather than inside the render loop because the
    order below needs the same answer, and asking twice would mean two places to
    keep in step.
  endcomment
  assign metal_row_index = -1
  assign swatch_flags = ''
  for option in product.options_with_values
    assign option_down = option.name | downcase
    assign is_swatch = false
    if option_down contains 'metal'
      assign is_swatch = true
      comment
        Recorded here, off the *same* needle the row loop's `is_metal` uses, so
        "the row that renders as the metal row" and "the row that is hoisted"
        are the same row by construction rather than by two tests agreeing.
      endcomment
      if metal_row_index == -1
        assign metal_row_index = forloop.index0
      endif
    else
      for value in option.values
        if value.swatch.color != blank or value.swatch.image != blank
          assign is_swatch = true
          break
        endif
      endfor
    endif
    if is_swatch
      assign swatch_flags = swatch_flags | append: '1|'
    else
      assign swatch_flags = swatch_flags | append: '0|'
    endif
  endfor
  assign swatch_flags = swatch_flags | split: '|'

  comment
    **The metal swatch row always leads.** The design's `axisDefs()` opens with
    `out.push({ key: 'metal', label: 'Metal', kind: 'swatch', primary: true })`
    and pushes everything else after it, so the metal is the first thing the
    panel offers. Rendering `product.options_with_values` in its own order gave
    whatever the merchant typed in admin instead — on this shop that put "Gold
    Karat" above "Metal".

    **The metal, and only the metal.** Hoisting every swatch-kind option instead
    would make the row order depend on admin metafield data: connect Shopify's
    Color metafield to a "Finish" or "Gemstone" option and the panel silently
    reorders, and if that option sits before Metal in admin it would *lead* —
    which the design never does. The design has exactly one swatch axis
    (`gj-variants.js:125` is the file's only `kind: 'swatch'`) and so states no
    precedence between two, and inventing one is not porting. Everything after
    the metal keeps the merchant's order.

    So this is a list of *positions* with the metal moved to the front.
    **The positions themselves are never renumbered, and that is load-bearing.**
    `data-qv-group`, `data-qv-prefix` and `current_variant.options[...]` are all
    indexed by the option's real place in `variant.options` — renumber them to
    the display order and the script would match a pick against the wrong axis
    and resolve the wrong variant, silently, for every product whose metal was
    not already first.

    With no metal option `metal_row_index` stays -1, the first branch is skipped
    and the order is simply 0…n-1.
  endcomment
  assign option_order = ''
  if metal_row_index != -1
    assign option_order = option_order | append: metal_row_index | append: '|'
  endif
  for option in product.options_with_values
    unless forloop.index0 == metal_row_index
      assign option_order = option_order | append: forloop.index0 | append: '|'
    endunless
  endfor
  assign option_order = option_order | split: '|'

  comment
    The design's `sc-if noVariants` row: with nothing to choose, the panel still
    says what the piece is made of. Only reached where the piece carries no metal
    option, so the metafield is the only thing left that knows — which is the
    card's rule too ("without a control the metafield still wins").
  endcomment
  assign metal_line = blank
  if metal_option == blank
    assign metal_line = product.metafields.custom.metal.value | append: ''
  endif

  comment
    The design's "Total carat weight" row, from the same metafield the card's
    caption reads.
  endcomment
  assign carat_label = product.metafields.custom.total_carat_weight.value | append: ''
  if carat_label != blank
    unless carat_label contains 'ct'
      assign carat_label = carat_label | append: ' ct'
    endunless
  endif

  comment
    Clarity, colour and certification are one value for the whole house in the
    design. They are theme settings here, with the matching product metafield in
    front of each so an exception can say so.
  endcomment
  assign clarity = product.metafields.custom.diamond_clarity_grade.value | default: settings.quick_view_clarity
  assign colour = product.metafields.custom.diamond_color_grade.value | default: settings.quick_view_colour
  assign certification = product.metafields.custom.certification_lab.value | default: settings.quick_view_certification

  comment
    Engraving is an axis in the design and a line item property here, so the
    input has to sit inside the form while the control it belongs to sits up in
    the rows with the others. `form=` is what associates the two — FormData
    collects it either way.
  endcomment
  assign supports_engraving = product.metafields.custom.engraving_available.value
  assign form_id = 'quick-view-form-' | append: product.id
  assign add_label = settings.quick_view_add_label | default: 'Add to Bag'
-%}

<div class="quick-view__grid">
  <div class="quick-view__gallery" data-quick-gallery{% if zoom_base != blank %} style="--qv-zoom-base: {{ zoom_base }};"{% endif %}>
    {%- if slide_count > 0 -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        One track, translated by whole slots — the design's transform carousel,
        not a stack of cross-fades. The card cross-fades; the quick view slides.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div
        class="quick-view__track"
        data-quick-track
        style="--qv-slides: {{ slide_count }};"
      >
        {%- for media in gallery_images -%}
          <div class="quick-view__slot" data-quick-slot="{{ forloop.index0 }}">
            <img
              class="quick-view__img"
              src="{{ media | image_url: width: 1100 }}"
              data-image-lqip="{{ media | image_url: width: 40 }}"
              srcset="{{ media | image_url: width: 500 }} 500w, {{ media | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w, {{ media | image_url: width: 1100 }} 1100w, {{ media | image_url: width: 1600 }} 1600w"
              sizes="(min-width: 55rem) 50vw, 95vw"
              alt="{{ media.alt | default: product.title | escape }}"
              width="{{ media.width }}"
              height="{{ media.height }}"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              draggable="false"
              data-quick-zoomable
            >
          </div>
        {%- endfor -%}

        {%- if gallery_video != blank -%}
          {%- assign mp4_sources = gallery_video.sources | where: 'format', 'mp4' -%}
          {%- assign mp4 = mp4_sources.last -%}
          {%- if mp4 != blank -%}
            <div class="quick-view__slot" data-quick-slot="{{ gallery_images.size }}" data-quick-spin>
              <video
                class="quick-view__img quick-view__video"
                data-src="{{ mp4.url }}"
                poster="{{ gallery_video.preview_image | image_url: width: 900 }}"
                width="{{ gallery_video.preview_image.width | default: 1240 }}"
                height="{{ gallery_video.preview_image.height | default: 827 }}"
                muted
                loop
                playsinline
                preload="none"
                aria-label="{{ 'products.spin_view' | t }}"
                data-video-lqip="{{ gallery_video.preview_image | image_url: width: 40 }}"
              ></video>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      {%- if slide_count > 1 -%}
        <button
          class="quick-view__arrow quick-view__arrow--prev"
          type="button"
          data-quick-step="-1"
          data-tip="{{ 'products.previous_view' | t }}"
          aria-label="{{ 'products.previous_view' | t }}"
        >
          <svg width="12" height="24" viewBox="0 0 12 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M10 2L2 12l8 10" />
          </svg>
        </button>
        <button
          class="quick-view__arrow quick-view__arrow--next"
          type="button"
          data-quick-step="1"
          data-tip="{{ 'products.next_view' | t }}"
          aria-label="{{ 'products.next_view' | t }}"
        >
          <svg width="12" height="24" viewBox="0 0 12 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M2 2l8 10-8 10" />
          </svg>
        </button>

        <div class="quick-view__dots">
          {%- for media in gallery_images -%}
            <button
              class="quick-view__dot{% if forloop.first %} is-on{% endif %}"
              type="button"
              data-quick-go="{{ forloop.index0 }}"
              data-tip="{{ 'products.go_to_view' | t }}"
              aria-label="{{ 'products.go_to_view' | t }}"
            ></button>
          {%- endfor -%}

          {%- if gallery_video != blank -%}
            <button
              class="quick-view__dot quick-view__dot--spin"
              type="button"
              data-quick-go="{{ gallery_images.size }}"
              data-tip="{{ 'products.spin_view' | t }}"
              aria-label="{{ 'products.spin_view' | t }}"
            >{{ 'products.spin_badge' | t }}</button>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The minimap the design shows while an image is zoomed: the whole
        photograph, with the part actually on screen framed in gold.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="quick-view__minimap" data-quick-minimap hidden>
        <span class="quick-view__minimap-view" data-quick-minimap-view></span>
      </span>
    {%- else -%}
      <div class="quick-view__slot">
        {{ 'product-1' | placeholder_svg_tag: 'placeholder-svg' }}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  <div class="quick-view__info">
    {%- if category != blank -%}
      <span class="quick-view__cat">{{ category }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}

    <h2 class="quick-view__title">{{ product.title }}</h2>

    {%- comment -%}
      The design's `priceLabel` is the price of the selection, not the range —
      every pick re-prices it. So this is the chosen variant's figure and the
      script rewrites it from Liquid's own money on every change.

      The note is a sibling of the figure rather than part of it, so a price
      change writes over the figure and leaves the note where it is. The card
      has to fold it into the captured markup for the same reason, being unable
      to split them there.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="quick-view__price">
      <s
        data-qv-compare
        {%- unless current_variant.compare_at_price > current_variant.price %} hidden{% endunless -%}
      >
        {%- if current_variant.compare_at_price > current_variant.price -%}
          {{- current_variant.compare_at_price | money -}}
        {%- endif -%}
      </s>
      <span data-qv-price>{{ current_variant.price | money }}</span>{% render 'tax-note' %}
    </div>

    {%- if has_options -%}
      {%- for position in option_order -%}
        {%- liquid
          comment
            The option's own position, not the loop's. See the note on
            `option_order` above: everything downstream — data-qv-group,
            data-qv-prefix, the variant matcher — is indexed by where the option
            really sits in `variant.options`, so the display order must never
            become the numbering.
          endcomment
          assign opt_index = position | plus: 0
          assign option = product.options_with_values[opt_index]
          assign selected_value = current_variant.options[opt_index]
          assign option_down = option.name | downcase

          assign is_metal = false
          if option_down contains 'metal'
            assign is_metal = true
          endif

          comment
            Which control an axis gets.

            The design fixes each axis's `kind` by hand because it owns its own
            axis table. Here they are the merchant's options, so the kind is
            derived — and the derivation reproduces the design's own arrangement
            on the design's own data: swatches for the metal, pills for the two-
            and three-value axes (karat, origin, cut, purchase option, backing,
            clasp), a select for the longer ones (ring size at thirteen, chain
            at five, wrist at four).

            Swatch or not was settled once, above, because the row order needs
            the same answer — `swatch_flags` is that answer, indexed the same
            way as everything else here.
          endcomment
          assign kind = 'select'
          if swatch_flags[opt_index] == '1'
            assign kind = 'swatch'
          elsif option.values.size <= 3
            assign kind = 'pill'
          endif

          assign swatch_values = option.values
          if is_metal
            assign swatch_values = ordered_metals
          endif

          comment
            The metal row's caption is the design's `metalName(v)` — "18k White
            Gold", the karat and the metal as one fact. The karat keeps its own
            row below, as it does in the design.
          endcomment
          assign value_label = selected_value
          assign compose_karat = false
          if is_metal and karat_option != blank
            assign compose_karat = true
            assign value_label = current_variant.options[karat_index] | append: ' ' | append: selected_value
          endif
        -%}

        <div class="quick-view__opt" data-qv-group="{{ opt_index }}">
          <div class="quick-view__opt-head">
            <span class="quick-view__opt-label">{{ option.name }}</span>
            <span
              class="quick-view__opt-value"
              data-qv-selected
              {%- if compose_karat %} data-qv-prefix="{{ karat_index }}"{% endif -%}
            >{{ value_label }}</span>
          </div>

          {%- case kind -%}
            {%- when 'swatch' -%}
              <div class="quick-view__swatches">
                {%- for value in swatch_values -%}
                  {%- liquid
                    assign value_text = value | append: ''

                    assign is_on = false
                    if value_text == selected_value
                      assign is_on = true
                    endif

                    comment
                      `ordered_metals` came back through `split`, so `value` is a
                      plain string and no longer the product_option_value drop.
                      The drop is what carries the merchant's native swatch, so
                      it is looked up again — without this the swatch snippet's
                      first and most specific branch is unreachable.
                    endcomment
                    assign value_drop = blank
                    for candidate in option.values
                      if candidate == value_text
                        assign value_drop = candidate
                        break
                      endif
                    endfor
                  -%}
                  <button
                    class="quick-view__swatch{% if is_on %} is-selected{% endif %}"
                    type="button"
                    data-qv-pick="{{ opt_index }}"
                    data-qv-value="{{ value_text | escape }}"
                    data-tip="{{ value_text | escape }}"
                    aria-label="{{ value_text | escape }}"
                    aria-pressed="{% if is_on %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
                  >
                    {%- comment -%}
                      `selected` is deliberately not passed: the button carries
                      the state and `.quick-view__swatch.is-selected .swatch-dot`
                      draws the ring from it, so a pick moves one class and
                      never leaves two rings behind.
                    {%- endcomment -%}
                    {% render 'metal-swatch', option_value: value_drop, size: 96 %}
                  </button>
                {%- endfor -%}
              </div>

            {%- when 'pill' -%}
              <div class="quick-view__pills">
                {%- for value in option.values -%}
                  {%- assign is_on = false -%}
                  {%- if value == selected_value -%}
                    {%- assign is_on = true -%}
                  {%- endif -%}
                  <button
                    class="quick-view__pill{% if is_on %} is-selected{% endif %}"
                    type="button"
                    data-qv-pick="{{ opt_index }}"
                    data-qv-value="{{ value | escape }}"
                    aria-pressed="{% if is_on %}true{% else %}false{% endif %}"
                  >{{ value }}</button>
                {%- endfor -%}
              </div>

            {%- else -%}
              <span class="quick-view__field">
                <select data-qv-select="{{ opt_index }}" aria-label="{{ option.name | escape }}">
                  {%- for value in option.values -%}
                    <option value="{{ value | escape }}" {% if value == selected_value %}selected{% endif %}>
                      {{- value -}}
                    </option>
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </select>
                <span class="quick-view__field-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                  <svg width="11" height="7" viewBox="0 0 11 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M1 1l4.5 4.5L10 1" />
                  </svg>
                </span>
              </span>
          {%- endcase -%}
        </div>
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if supports_engraving -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        An axis in the design, a line item property here — so it looks like the
        others and posts like a property. The input is associated with the form
        by `form=` rather than by nesting, and stays disabled until it is asked
        for so an empty engraving never reaches the bag.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="quick-view__opt" data-qv-engrave>
        <div class="quick-view__opt-head">
          <span class="quick-view__opt-label">{{ 'products.engraving' | t }}</span>
          <span class="quick-view__opt-value" data-qv-engrave-value>{{ 'products.engraving_none' | t }}</span>
        </div>

        <div class="quick-view__pills">
          <button
            class="quick-view__pill is-selected"
            type="button"
            data-qv-engrave-pick="off"
            aria-pressed="true"
          >{{ 'products.engraving_none' | t }}</button>
          <button
            class="quick-view__pill"
            type="button"
            data-qv-engrave-pick="on"
            aria-pressed="false"
          >{{ 'products.engraving_custom' | t }}</button>
        </div>

        <input
          class="quick-view__engrave-field"
          type="text"
          form="{{ form_id }}"
          name="properties[Custom Engraving]"
          maxlength="20"
          autocomplete="off"
          placeholder="{{ 'products.custom_engraving_placeholder' | t | escape }}"
          aria-label="{{ 'products.custom_engraving' | t | escape }}"
          data-qv-engrave-field
          hidden
          disabled
        >

        <p class="quick-view__opt-note">{{ 'products.custom_engraving_hint' | t }}</p>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    <dl class="quick-view__specs">
      {%- comment -%}
        What the piece is, rather than what can be chosen about it. The metal
        only appears here where nothing above states it — with a metal option
        the row above is the metal, exactly as the design's `sc-if noVariants`
        has it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if metal_line != blank -%}
        <div class="quick-view__spec">
          <dt>{{ 'products.metal' | t }}</dt>
          <dd>{{ metal_line }}</dd>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if carat_label != blank -%}
        <div class="quick-view__spec">
          <dt>{{ 'products.carat_weight' | t }}</dt>
          <dd>{{ carat_label }}</dd>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if clarity != blank -%}
        <div class="quick-view__spec">
          <dt>{{ 'products.clarity' | t }}</dt>
          <dd>{{ clarity }}</dd>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if colour != blank -%}
        <div class="quick-view__spec">
          <dt>{{ 'products.colour' | t }}</dt>
          <dd>{{ colour }}</dd>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if certification != blank -%}
        <div class="quick-view__spec">
          <dt>{{ 'products.certification' | t }}</dt>
          <dd>{{ certification }}</dd>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </dl>

    {%- comment -%}
      One button, always — the rows above are what choose the piece now, so the
      form no longer needs a picker of its own and the state of the selection is
      said on the button. Both labels ride on it as attributes so the swap needs
      no translation string in the script.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- form 'product', product, id: form_id, class: 'quick-view__form', data-cart-add: '' -%}
      <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ current_variant.id }}" data-qv-variant-id>

      <button
        class="quick-view__add"
        type="submit"
        name="add"
        data-qv-add
        data-add-label="{{ add_label | escape }}"
        data-sold-out-label="{{ 'products.sold_out' | t | escape }}"
        data-unavailable-label="{{ 'products.unavailable' | t | escape }}"
        aria-disabled="{% if current_variant.available %}false{% else %}true{% endif %}"
        {% unless current_variant.available %}disabled{% endunless %}
      >
        {%- if current_variant.available -%}
          {{ add_label }}
        {%- else -%}
          {{ 'products.sold_out' | t }}
        {%- endif -%}
      </button>
    {%- endform -%}

    {%- if settings.quick_view_reassurance != blank -%}
      <p class="quick-view__reassurance">
        <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M10 2.4 4 4.7v5c0 3.4 2.4 6.4 6 7.9 3.6-1.5 6-4.5 6-7.9v-5Z" />
          <path d="M7.4 9.9 9.3 11.8 12.9 8.1" />
        </svg>
        <span>{{ settings.quick_view_reassurance }}</span>
      </p>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if has_options -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        What the rows resolve against. Every price in it is Liquid's `money`, so
        a pick never asks the browser to format a figure — the same contract the
        product template works to.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <script type="application/json" data-qv-variants>
        [
          {%- for variant in product.variants -%}
            {
              "id": {{ variant.id | json }},
              "available": {{ variant.available | json }},
              "options": {{ variant.options | json }},
              "price": {{ variant.price | money | json }},
              "compareAtPrice": {% if variant.compare_at_price > variant.price %}{{ variant.compare_at_price | money | json }}{% else %}""{% endif %}
            }{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
          {%- endfor -%}
        ]
      </script>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

<a class="quick-view__full" href="{{ product.url }}">
  <span>{{ 'products.view_full_details' | t }}</span>
  <span class="quick-view__full-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
</a>
